// Input-parser fixture: arity 3, mixed element types, nested one level deeper
// (ADR-092, REP-54).
//
// ``sections(lines(`{int},{int},{text}`))`` is `Vec[Vec[(Int, Int, Text)]]`.
// Three things at once, deliberately: arity is not 2, the elements are not all
// `Int`, and the tuple sits two collections deep, so a fix that answered the
// *first child's* descriptor, or that only reached one level of nesting, is
// red here and green in `template_multi_capture_tuple`.
//
// OBSERVED RED at 6e74642 (the fix removed): line 1 printed
// `[[Unit, Unit], [Unit]]`.
//
// **Line 2 prints the same on both binaries and must not change.** That is the
// fixture's own evidence for what the defect was: the value was always a real
// tuple — `walk_template` assembled it with the right `TupleSchema` — and only
// the *collection's element tag* lied about it. Reaching an element by
// `.get(...)` reads the descriptor from the object's own header and prints
// `(3, 4, beta)`; rendering the collection reads the tag and printed `Unit`.
// If line 2 ever starts changing with line 1, the fix has moved from the tag
// to the value and something else is wrong.
fn main() -> Int {
    var groups = read sections(lines(`{int},{int},{text}`));
    out(groups);
    out(groups.get(0).get(1));
    groups.len()
}
